    <!--!!!!!!!!!!!! Lidar !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!-->
    <!--
      lidar_top is the Livox MID360 / MID360s itself: the origin its points are
      reported in, and the frame livox_ros_driver2 stamps livox/lidar with. So
      lidar_top_joint is the mount extrinsic measured to that point (by hand
      today, good to a cm or two); the 0.25 rad pitch is the physical lidar
      tilt. Safe to publish statically now that pointlio broadcasts its own
      pointlio_odom -> pointlio_body branch and no longer parents any frame of
      the robot tree.

      Until 2026-10 lidar_top stood for two points at once: syncai_lio_bridge
      mapped the Point-LIO body pose through it as if it were the IMU, while
      the raw cloud was stamped with a separate <robot_id>/laser that nothing
      parented. The lidar and its IMU are t_il (~5 cm) apart, so they are two
      links now: lidar_top for the lidar, lidar_top_imu below for the IMU.
    -->
    <link name="lidar_top"/>
    <joint name="lidar_top_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="base_link"/>
        <child link="lidar_top"/>
        <origin xyz="0.195 0.00 0.155" rpy="0 0.25 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!--
      The lidar's built-in IMU: the static counterpart of
      <robot_id>/pointlio_body, because Point-LIO's body frame is the IMU, not
      the lidar (it broadcasts the IMU pose, and puts every point into the IMU
      frame through r_il / t_il). It is the frame to map the Point-LIO body pose
      onto base_link through, i.e. what syncai_lio_bridge's and
      syncai_robot_state's lidar_frame should name; both still name lidar_top
      until their own repositories move to this one, which keeps the ~5 cm
      error of before but nothing worse.

      The offset mirrors syncai_pointlio's params/pointlio_params.yaml rather
      than the Livox datasheet on its own: this joint has to be the exact
      inverse of the extrinsic Point-LIO runs with, or mapping pointlio_body
      back through it misses lidar_top by the difference. With r_il = identity
      the inverse is xyz = -t_il, rpy = 0. Change the two together. t_il is the
      MID360's published IMU offset; whether the MID360s shares it has not been
      checked, and if it does not, pointlio_params.yaml is what is wrong and
      this joint follows it.
    -->
    <link name="lidar_top_imu"/>
    <joint name="lidar_top_imu_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="lidar_top"/>
        <child link="lidar_top_imu"/>
        <origin xyz="0.011 0.02329 -0.04412" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!--!!!!!!!!!!!! Camera !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!-->
    <!--
      Camera mount extrinsic, robot01's (2026-10-20): translation hand-measured,
      rotation calibrated. docs/lidar-camera-calibration.md has the procedure
      and the evidence; this file is one URDF for the whole fleet, so a second
      robot with a camera needs a per-robot override, which does not exist yet.

      xyz is the tape measure: camera 7 cm ahead of and 6.5 cm below the lidar
      along the body axes, expressed in lidar_top, which is pitched 0.25 rad
      down: R_y(0.25)^T * (0.07, 0, -0.065). Good to 1-2 cm. It was NOT taken
      from the calibration on purpose: on these scenes (mostly 2-6 m away) the
      camera height trades against its tilt, and the 6-DoF fits moved it 4-27
      cm in directions that disagreed between bags, with the robot lying 1.2 m
      in front of the camera mis-projected by ~100 px under the worst of them.

      rpy is a rotation-only NID fit of the lidar intensity to the image
      (scripts/calibration/nid_fit.py) on two static bags at different headings,
      pos01 + pos02, with camera0.yaml's equidistant intrinsics of 2026-10-20.
      Against the tape measure's level optical frame (-pi/2 + 0.25, 0, -pi/2) it
      is pan right 0.81 deg (each bag alone 0.73 / 0.89), tilt down 0.18 deg,
      roll -0.56 deg (tilt and roll are coupled and good to ~0.3 deg, ~3 px at
      the image edge). It cut the image-edge vs lidar-edge residual from
      9-11 px to 4-5 px horizontally on each bag with the other bag's rotation.
      Refit it whenever camera0.yaml changes: the principal point and the pan
      trade against each other.

      Parented to lidar_top rather than base_link so the camera stays rigidly
      tied to the lidar when the lidar mount extrinsic is re-measured; the
      calibration solves camera <-> lidar, not camera <-> body. lidar_top is the
      frame the raw cloud is stamped with, i.e. the "lidar" of that calibration,
      so its T_lidar_camera goes into this joint as it is, and not under
      lidar_top_imu. camera_optical_frame follows the REP 103 optical
      convention (z forward, x right, y down); it is the frame_id
      syncai_camera_feeder stamps on image_raw/compressed.
    -->
    <link name="camera_optical_frame"/>
    <joint name="camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="lidar_top"/>
        <child link="camera_optical_frame"/>
        <origin xyz="0.084 0.0 -0.046" rpy="-1.3240 -0.0060 -1.5868"/>
    </joint>
</robot>